# Database URL.
# - RAILWAY (production): PostgreSQL (auto-provided by Railway's PostgreSQL add-on)
#   Just reference the database variable, e.g.  DATABASE_URL=${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}
# - LOCAL DEV: run PostgreSQL locally (e.g. via Docker)
#     postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/cliperpro
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/cliperpro"

# OpenAI API key (optional here — can also be set in the app's Settings UI)
OPENAI_API_KEY=""

# OpenAI model override (optional). Defaults to gpt-4o-mini
OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# YOUTUBE COOKIES (REQUIRED for video download/render feature on cloud servers)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# YouTube blocks cloud server IPs (Railway, Heroku, AWS) as bots.
# To make video downloads work, you MUST provide YouTube cookies from your
# logged-in browser session.
#
# HOW TO GET COOKIES:
#   1. Install a browser extension: "Get cookies.txt LOCALLY"
#      (Chrome: https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/get-cookiestxt-locally/...)
#   2. Log into YouTube (youtube.com) in your browser
#   3. Click the extension icon → export cookies for youtube.com
#   4. You'll get a file like "youtube.com_cookies.txt" in Netscape format
#   5. Base64-encode that file:
#        macOS/Linux: base64 youtube.com_cookies.txt
#        Windows:    use https://www.base64encode.org/
#   6. Copy the entire base64 string
#   7. Set it as the YOUTUBE_COOKIES env var on Railway:
#        YOUTUBE_COOKIES=PD94bWwgdmVyc2lvbj0iMS4wIiBlbmNvZGluZz0...
#
# NOTE: Cookies expire! Re-export them every 1-2 months or when downloads
#       stop working.
# WARNING: Do NOT share your cookies file — it gives access to your YouTube
#          account. Treat it like a password.
YOUTUBE_COOKIES=""
